# KNOWN GAP — Core #8, BOTH LANES BROKEN IN DIFFERENT WAYS. `gg check`
# accepts; the Rust reference ICEs; the self-host compiles and then
# double-frees at runtime.
#
#   gg check               : exit 0
#   gg build (C)           : exit 101 ICE, move-follow-through violation,
#                            src/ir/lowering/mod.rs:1872
#   gg build --backend=llvm: exit 101, identical
#   self-host              : COMPILES, then exit 134,
#                            `free(): double free detected in tcache 2`
#
# INTENDED: `hello world-forced!`, exit 0.
#
# WHY THIS ONE MATTERS BEYOND THE ICE: it is the measured answer to the Track B
# reachability question -- a DROPPABLE inner does reach the `shared` lock-chain,
# so the self-host's `shared`-read path mints an alias of guarded memory and
# registers it for drop.
#
# ⚠ THE DISCRIMINATOR IS *NOT* THE OWNING DESTINATION. Measured: the
# non-owning read (`print(s)`) double-frees identically to the owning read
# (`String snap = s; print(snap)`). So this is NOT the
# consume-position/OpMove-on-a-shallow-alias mechanism. The trigger is the
# `spawn` / `&`-param TASK-FACADE path -- the `__get_ptr` + GIDeref(wr_binding)
# re-seed, which unlike the plain lock-chain reads IS registered for drop.
# Tagging the plain `shared`-read sites as views would not touch this.
#
# Without the spawn, the self-host is CLEAN on the same shape (`shared String
# s = "a"+"b"` then `s = s + "!"` then print -> exit 0, `ab!`). The spawn is
# load-bearing; keep it in any minimization.
#
# Related, same seam, filed separately: the `&`-param family (a `Mutex[T]`
# behind a `&` param loses writes, SEGVs, and deadlocks on the second lock).
# All of these are borrows of resource-owned memory crossing an ownership
# boundary.
#
# Graduate out of known_gaps/ (un-ignore + move) the round this is fixed.

async void bump(String &s):
    s = s + "!"

async void main():
    shared String s = "hello " + "world" + "-forced"
    Task[void] t = spawn bump(&s)
    t.await()
    print(s)
